<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shaka (Zulu king)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1787–<a href="/years/1828">1828</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shaka was the king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> who built the Zulu kingdom into a powerful military state in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from the 1810s. His wars and state-building are associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mfecane">Mfecane</a>, a period of upheaval and migration across southern Africa. Groups displaced in the Mfecane invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwean plateau</a> and destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi</a> state.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…factor was the Mfecane, the cascade of military displacement associated with the rise of the Zulu kingdom under <b>Shaka</b> from the 1810s, which sent successive Nguni-speaking groups across the plateau, among them Zwangendaba's Ngoni in the early 1830s and the Swati followers of Queen Nyamazana Dlamini, whose forces…</blockquote><a href="/empire/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Sobhuza combined force with diplomacy: he married Thandile, a daughter of Zwide, who later became an influential queen mother, and he avoided open war with the rising Zulu Kingdom of <b>Shaka</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swazi-kingdom">Swazi Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Shaka</b>, a son of the Zulu chief Senzangakhona born around 1787, grew up in exile and rose as a warrior in Dingiswayo’s army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mpondo grew powerful in the early 19th century under Faku, who survived raids by <b>Shaka</b>'s Zulu Kingdom and absorbed refugees from the wars of that era.</blockquote><a href="/state/mpondo-kingdom">Mpondo Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Shaka</b> served in his army and, with Dingiswayo's backing, became ruler of the Zulu, a Mthethwa dependent.</blockquote><a href="/state/mthethwa-paramountcy">Mthethwa Paramountcy</a></li><li><blockquote>Mzilikazi, a Khumalo commander under <b>Shaka</b>, broke away around 1822 with a few hundred followers and moved onto the highveld, absorbing Sotho-Tswana communities as he went.</blockquote><a href="/state/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
